# An iron pickaxe is three iron ingots and two sticks at a placed crafting
# table. The frames decide the shape: get_iron_pickaxe -> get_raw_iron (three
# frames through find_ore -> search_surface) and -> get_iron_ingot, get_sticks
# and get_crafting_table_near (one frame each), so the deepest chain is four,
# the core cap.
#
# What it deliberately does not fetch: a furnace. `get_furnace` is four frames
# by itself (get_furnace -> get_cobblestone -> get_wooden_pickaxe -> get_planks),
# so calling it from here would be five and the loader rejects the file. The
# cheapest restructure is the one `get_iron_ingot` already documents: the ladder
# is cumulative, the furnace, coal and raw iron come first, and this skill
# carries only the rung its own chain is missing. Raw iron does fit
# (`get_raw_iron` is three frames), so a body with a stone pickaxe really does
# reach its first iron pickaxe through this one call; fuel and the furnace do
# not, so a body without them gets `get_iron_ingot`'s own kinds (no_fuel,
# no_furnace) after its three tries, and the caller decides what to get first.
#
# Why the rules are ordered this way: the goal check is on top, then the craft,
# so a body that already smelted three ingots and holds two sticks only has to
# stand at its table. Raw iron comes before smelting, so a missing ore is one
# `get_raw_iron` call and not three failed smelts, and its kind (`needs_tool`
# without a stone pickaxe, `not_found` when the search runs out) is then the
# one this skill fails with. `get_iron_ingot(3)` cooks one ingot per call and
# is re-selected until three are held; it places the furnace the body carries,
# and a body without fuel or a furnace fails this skill with that kind after
# three tries. Sticks come before the table rule because a table is made of
# planks and `get_sticks` may spend the last ones. The table rule is last: it
# fires only when the ingots and sticks are ready and no table stands in reach.
#
# Surplus ore: the raw iron guard asks for three however many ingots are
# already held, because the DSL has no subtraction; one extra ore in the pack
# costs a few ticks and feeds the next rung.
skill get_iron_pickaxe()
  doc "Have an iron pickaxe: three iron ingots and two sticks crafted at a placed crafting table, fetching the raw iron, the sticks and the table itself."
  rules:
    count(iron_pickaxe) >= 1 -> done
    count(iron_ingot) >= 3 and count(stick) >= 2 and near(crafting_table, 4) -> craft(iron_pickaxe)
    count(iron_ingot) < 3 and count(raw_iron) < 3 -> do get_raw_iron(3)
    count(iron_ingot) < 3 -> do get_iron_ingot(3)
    count(stick) < 2 -> do get_sticks(2)
    count(iron_ingot) >= 3 and count(stick) >= 2 and not near(crafting_table, 4) -> do get_crafting_table_near()
